From ed4f2691d9ee514ce5276ebe70f60a85c5593d0e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lucas Santos <lsantos7654@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 1 May 2026 01:06:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 27/27] Switch listener from @hono/node-server to native
 Bun.serve
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The disconnect-detection chase (patches 0021–0026) all targeted
the wrong substrate. @hono/node-server runs Hono on Bun's Node
http compat. On that path:

  - `c.req.raw.signal` doesn't fire reliably for long-lived SSE
    responses. The adaptor wires it from `outgoing.on("close")`,
    which Bun's Node compat batches.
  - `outgoing.destroyed` and friends update only when that batched
    event is processed.
  - Active write probes don't surface anything because clean FIN
    closes the read side; the kernel happily accepts more writes
    into the send buffer.
  - Read-side flags (`socket.readable`, `socket.readableEnded`)
    are also cached against the same batched events.

User confirmed Phase A (read-side flag check) didn't fix it.
Escalating to Phase B from the plan: native `Bun.serve`.

Native Bun.serve runs Hono on Bun's uWebSockets path. There,
`req.signal.abort()` fires from `RequestContext.onAbort` via
`us_socket_context_on_close` synchronously when the kernel
delivers EPOLLRDHUP — no batching, no wait for "other I/O".
This is the substrate the Bun expert pointed us at originally.

Migration:
  - Replace `createNodeWebSocket` with `createBunWebSocket`
    from `hono/bun`. Same `upgradeWebSocket` shape; existing
    PtyRoutes consumer is unchanged.
  - Replace `createAdaptorServer({ fetch })` +
    `server.listen(port, host)` with `Bun.serve({ fetch,
    websocket, port, hostname })`.
  - Pass `server` as second arg to `app.fetch(req, server)` so
    Hono's `getBunServer(c)` can find it for WS upgrades.
  - Bun.Server has a different shape than node:http.Server:
    `server.port` instead of `server.address()`, `server.stop()`
    instead of `server.close(cb)`. Listener interface stays
    identical.
  - Drop @hono/node-server import; remove the type import for
    `ServerType`.

In `event.ts`:
  - Drop `c.env.incoming/outgoing` access — that was the
    @hono/node-server-specific bindings shape.
  - Drop `incoming.on/once`, `outgoing.on/once`,
    `socket.setKeepAlive` — these don't apply on Bun.serve.
  - Drop the entire module-level `_sweep` `setInterval`. Native
    Bun.serve fires the disconnect signal synchronously, so the
    sweep is no longer load-bearing. Removing it simplifies the
    code and removes the timer overhead.
  - Keep `stream.onAbort` and `c.req.raw.signal.addEventListener`
    as the two cleanup paths. Both are wired to the same uSockets
    source on Bun.serve.

In real-server tests:
  - Replace `createAdaptorServer` with `Bun.serve` to match
    production. Drop the 11s heartbeat-wait — disconnect now
    fires within milliseconds, so 200ms slack is enough.

8/8 event tests pass, including the cross-process curl-kill
scenario. With Bun.serve as the substrate, neither the periodic
sweep nor the active probe is necessary; cleanup is event-driven
and instant.

